{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_dny4wld6qt5v4ojaom3lgx6y6p33ulg6jzpg4afg22ddxdtdctka","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The build succeeds with no errors or warnings. Here's a summary of the translation:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe C source uses `ucontext.h` (`getcontext`/`makecontext`/`swapcontext`) to implement stackful coroutines that share one big \"running stack\" buffer in the schedule, with each suspended coroutine's stack saved/restored on yield/resume.\n\nSince Rust has no safe equivalent to `ucontext` and rule 4 forbids FFI to libc, I translated the semantics using OS threads coordinated by mutex/condvar handshakes — one OS thread per live coroutine, with the main thread blocking on a response condvar while the coroutine runs, and the coroutine blocking on a command condvar while suspended. This preserves the public behavior:\n\n- `coroutine_open` allocates a `Schedule` with a `STACK_SIZE` byte buffer (kept for layout fidelity even though Rust threads don't share it) and a slot table sized to `DEFAULT_COROUTINE`.\n- `coroutine_new` mirrors the C grow-or-find-slot logic, doubling the slot vector when full and otherwise scanning starting at `(i + nco) % cap`.\n- `coroutine_resume` spawns a thread for `READY` coroutines, sets `running`/`RUNNING`, and waits for either a `Yielded` or `Done` response. On `Done` it joins the thread, drops the coroutine, decrements `nco`, and resets `running` to `-1`, exactly like the C `mainfunc` cleanup.\n- `coroutine_yield` flips status to `SUSPEND`, clears `running`, signals the main thread, and blocks until told to resume (or to die, if the schedule is being torn down).\n- `coroutine_status` and `coroutine_running` are direct field reads, with the same `DEAD`/slot-empty handling as C.\n- `Drop` for `Coroutine` and `coroutine_close` together ensure any still-suspended coroutine threads are unblocked (via a `Kill` command that panics out of the user closure inside `catch_unwind`) and joined before memory is released.\n\nThe only `unsafe` is the minimum needed to share `*mut Schedule`/`*mut Coroutine` with the worker thread; mutual exclusion is enforced by the resume/yield handshake so only one thread ever dereferences them at a time. No `libc`/FFI is used, no `unimplemented!`/`todo!`/`panic!` placeholders remain, and `cargo build` succeeds cleanly."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":186,"cache_read_input_tokens":29056,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":186,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1071c3c8-f75f-44e3-a194-e757e3214967","uuid":"acbfaa16-d4f9-4efd-8fed-024f1c850e79"}
